## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you empirically determine the optimal threshold size?
- What techniques would you use to verify the algorithm's stability?
- How does this hybrid approach perform on partially sorted data?
- What modifications would be needed for parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to apply k-way merge algorithms to partially sorted data, a common scenario in data processing pipelines and external sorting. It emphasizes understanding how the number of runs (r) impacts both time complexity and memory usage, connecting theoretical concepts with practical implementation.

## Key Concepts

- **k-way merge**: Efficient merging of multiple sorted sequences
- **Run detection**: Identifying sorted segments in a single linear pass
- **Min-heap**: Priority queue for selecting minimum elements efficiently
- **Complexity analysis**: O(n log r) time, O(r) auxiliary space
- **Edge cases**: Empty input, duplicate elements, and single-run scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor() { 
    this.heap = []; 
  }

  size() { 
    return this.heap.length; 
  }

  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this._heapifyUp(this.heap.length - 1);
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return null;
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const last = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = last;
      this._heapifyDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }

  _heapifyUp(index) { /* implementation */ }
  _heapifyDown(index) { /* implementation */ }
}

function kWayMerge(arr) {
  // Edge case: empty input
  if (arr.length === 0) return [];

  // Detect runs
  const runs = [];
  let start = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    if (i === arr.length || arr[i-1] > arr[i]) {
      runs.push({start, end: i-1, index: start});
      start = i;
    }
  }

  // Edge case: r=1 (already sorted)
  if (runs.length === 1) return arr.slice();

  // Initialize heap with first elements
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  const result = [];

  runs.forEach((run, i) => {
    heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId: i});
  });

  // Merge process
  while (heap.size() > 0) {
    const {value, runId} = heap.pop();
    result.push(value);

    const run = runs[runId];
    if (run.index < run.end) {
      run.index++;
      heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId});
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

