## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of prefix-based searching using a practical, lightweight data structure. It combines Trie fundamentals with per-node augmentation to enable fast prefix queries in real time—useful in autocomplete systems, IP routing, and spell checking.

## Key Concepts
- **Trie structure**: Each node contains 26 children (one per letter) and maintains a pass counter representing how many words pass through that node
- **insert operation**: O(m) time, where m is word length; creates nodes as needed and increments pass at each node visited
- **count_with_prefix operation**: O(k) time, where k is prefix length; traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass counter
- **Handling duplicates**: Pass counters naturally accumulate, so duplicate insertions simply increment the counter without special handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.pass = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) node.next[idx] = new TrieNode();
      node.pass++;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    node.pass++;
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) return 0;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    return node.pass;
  }
}

// Example usage
const trie = new Trie();
['cat', 'car', 'cart', 'dog', 'dot'].forEach(w => trie.insert(w));
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('ca')); // 3
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('car')); // 1
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('d')); // 2
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('do')); // 2
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates dynamic programming skills with state expansion, requiring candidates to track whether the cell removal has been used. It tests proper handling of grid boundary conditions and provides opportunities to discuss space optimization strategies and compare memoization versus tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with auxiliary state tracking
- Space optimization using rolling arrays
- Boundary condition handling
- State transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPathWithOneRemoval(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));

  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i-1][j]);
      }
      if (j > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i][j-1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.min(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
- Time: O(m × n) - we process each cell once
- Space: O(m × n) - can be optimized to O(n) using rolling arrays since we only need the previous row

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DP with a multi‑dimensional state and non‑trivial transitions, mixing path constraints with exact move counts. It also probes memoization vs bottom‑up, boundary handling, and memory optimizations essential for large grids.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) representing position and moves
- Transitions from (i-1,j,t-1), (i,j-1,t-1), (i-1,j-1,t-1)
- -INF for invalid states; base cases
- Memory optimization via rolling arrays
- Trade-offs between readability (memoized DFS) and performance (tabulation)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridSum(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array.from({length:m}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  function dfs(i,j,t){
    if(i<0||j<0||t<0) return NEG;
    if(i===0&&j===0&&t===0) return grid[0][0];
    if(memo[i][j][t]!==NEG) return memo[i][j][t];
    const best = Math.max(dfs(i-1,j,t-1), dfs(i,j-1,t-1), dfs(i-1,j-1,t-1));
    memo[i][j][t] = (best===NEG)? NEG : grid[i][j] + best;
    return memo[i][j][t];
  }
  return dfs(n-1,m-1,k);
}
```

